            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Russian for to catch fire, then the Russian for a fire extinguisher."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: спасатель",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "спасатель (spasátel) — \"a rescuer, a lifeguard\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Спасатели приехали через десять минут. Spasáteli priyékhali chérez désyat minút. The rescuers arrived ten minutes later."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Genitive спасателя."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "спасатель (spasátel) is built on спасать (spasát) \"to save\", from спасти, Old East Slavic sŭpasti (EtymDB), the verb also behind спасибо, with the ending -тель for a person."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
